translation:
Target is the proposition asserting that for all arbitrary types Fm (sentences), S₀, and S (proof systems), equipped with formal logical connectives (🡒, ⋏, ⋎, ∼) and constants (⊤, ⊥) on Fm and provability relations for S₀ and S over Fm, and for every system 𝓑 : S₀, every system 𝓢 : S (assuming both 𝓑 and 𝓢 prove all classical propositional tautologies and are closed under modus ponens), and every function K : Fm → Fm, if the following four hypotheses hold:
1. (Def1) For every sentence φ : Fm, if 𝓑 proves φ, then 𝓢 proves K φ;
2. (Def2) For all sentences φ, ψ : Fm, 𝓢 proves K (φ 🡒 ψ) 🡒 (K φ 🡒 K ψ);
3. (Def3) For every sentence φ : Fm, 𝓢 proves (K φ) 🡒 φ;
4. (Def4) For every sentence φ : Fm, 𝓢 proves K ((K φ) 🡒 φ);
then for every sentence p : Fm, 𝓢 proves ∼(K ((K (∼p)) 🡘 p)), i.e., the formal negation of K applied to the biconditional ((K (∼p)) 🡒 p) ⋏ (p 🡒 (K (∼p))).

Notable absences: Def5 (the existence of a fixed point in 𝓑) is not assumed, even though it appears in the context; neither 𝓑 nor 𝓢 is assumed to be consistent; 𝓢 is not assumed to satisfy necessitation (𝓢 ⊢ φ → 𝓢 ⊢ K φ) or to coincide with or extend 𝓑 beyond Def1; and p is universally quantified over all sentences in Fm rather than being restricted to a special or fixed-point sentence.

